  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 1209 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances will increase near or shortly\nafter sunrise Saturday, but confidence remains low in coverage and\nlocation of the best storm chances. Thus have stuck with the NBM\ndepiction of the highest pops across northeast Oklahoma during the\nmorning, with activity spreading south and east while diminishing\nin coverage by early afternoon. Temperatures are also problematic\nSaturday, and again the NBM looks like a good first guess, with\nhighs in the 90s to near 100 degrees. Afternoon heat index values\nmay reach near 105 degrees in some places Saturday afternoon, but\nconfidence is not high enough to issue a heat advisory at this\ntime.\n\nAnother chance of showers and thunderstorms will exist Saturday\nevening into early Sunday as storms that fire along a weak surface\nboundary just to our north may move south into the area. Northeast\nOklahoma and northwest Arkansas are most likely to see storm\nimpacts during this time frame.\n\nBy Sunday, an upper ridge builds back more strongly over the area\nending most precipitation chances. Unseasonably hot temperatures\nwill prevail Sunday through at least the middle of next week, and\nheat headlines may be required for some locations each day.\n\nA cold front may move into the area by late next week bringing\ncooler temperatures and increasing shower and thunderstorm\nchances, but confidence in the timing of this front remains low.",
